model {
  extend basalt.poolRuntime {
    metadata {
      constraint "Generation sends own submission tracking. Accepted HTTP responses and attempted WebSocket generation writes make later local failures terminal unknown; only positively validated provider refusals permit failover. Credential, refresh and token-count calls remain auxiliary. Preserve native error envelopes, correlation identity, pre-write recovery and retained Home funding behavior. Gemini zero-output responses retain the upstream credential cooldown exemption, but submission ownership suppresses upstream credential rotation after acceptance because the generation outcome is unknown. Model providers whose generation sends use the shared tracked HTTP client are guarded by that transport even without provider-specific refusal validation; their in-band errors after acceptance stay terminal unknown and never fail over. A buffered overload rejection inherits refusal validation only from its original stream event. A model-call plugin's generation send keeps the tracked transport when Home dispatches to it, after the plugin's availability check. A Home-granted local plugin runs outside the conductor, so its call carries its own submission tracking: a cut accepted answer ends as the unknown outcome and the plugin's retry never reaches the provider. A Home that cannot grant local plugins answers unsupported, and the node takes credential dispatch instead. Each provider path where upstream rotates an accepted-then-cut generation to another credential has a named regression that expects one send and the unknown outcome. This behaviour is permanent: upstream fails over after an accepted send on purpose, so the carry is re-cut at every pin move and never retires."
    }
  }
}
